**Problem.** Every keystroke firing a request wastes bandwidth and server load, and responses can arrive out of order: the reply for 'pho' might land after the reply for 'phone' and overwrite it with stale results.

**Solution outline.**
1. Keep `query` in state, updated on every change.
2. Derive a **debounced** value (wait about 300 ms of no typing).
3. In an effect keyed on the debounced value, skip if its trimmed length is under 3 (clear results).
4. Otherwise create an `AbortController`, call `fetch(url, { signal })`, and in the effect **cleanup** call `controller.abort()`. When the query changes, the previous request is cancelled, so it can never set state.
5. Ignore `AbortError` in the catch block; set an error state for other failures.

```jsx
function useDebounce(value, delay = 300) {
  const [v, setV] = useState(value);
  useEffect(() => {
    const t = setTimeout(() => setV(value), delay);
    return () => clearTimeout(t);
  }, [value, delay]);
  return v;
}

function Search() {
  const [q, setQ] = useState('');
  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);
  const [status, setStatus] = useState('idle');
  const dq = useDebounce(q.trim());
  useEffect(() => {
    if (dq.length < 3) { setItems([]); setStatus('idle'); return; }
    const ctrl = new AbortController();
    setStatus('loading');
    fetch(`https://dummyjson.com/products/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(dq)}`, { signal: ctrl.signal })
      .then(r => { if (!r.ok) throw new Error(r.status); return r.json(); })
      .then(d => { setItems(d.products); setStatus('done'); })
      .catch(e => { if (e.name !== 'AbortError') setStatus('error'); });
    return () => ctrl.abort();
  }, [dq]);
  // render: a controlled <input> bound to q and setQ, then a status line
  // (Loading, error, or No results) and a list of items using key={p.id}
  return renderSearchUI(q, setQ, status, items);
}
```

**Why it works.** Debounce reduces request count; abort in cleanup guarantees only the latest query can update state, which solves the race. An alternative is a request-id ref compared before `setState`, but aborting also frees network resources.

**Extras:** an in-memory cache keyed by query, keyboard navigation with ARIA combobox roles, and highlighting matched text.
